{
  "dom_id": "step-q117803",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2$ at its local maximum points?",
  "worked_solution": "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=1$ are **local minimums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x.$  For now, we're interested in the local minimums (not local maximums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -3+2 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-1$ at its local maximum points.",
